---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Stub from '../components/Stub.astro';
import { machines } from '../data/machines';
---
<Base title="Support · Lemus" description="Lemus plugins: user guides, the ROM folder, panel artwork, the update check, the forum and contact." current="support">
<Stub heading="Support">
<p>What all Lemus plugins share. Each plugin's own controls are in its user guide; installing is on the <a href="/download#installing">download page</a>.</p>

<h2 id="guides" class="h3">User guides</h2>
<ul>
{machines.map((m) => <li><a href={`/${m.slug}/guide`} class="mono">{m.slug}</a>: {m.emulates}</li>)}
</ul>

<h2 id="rom" class="h3">ROM images</h2>
<p>A machine's firmware is its maker's copyrighted work, so Lemus doesn't include it. A machine with a CPU runs it from your own ROM image; its guide names the file and its SHA1. Without it, a machine marked [~] plays its voice over MIDI but nothing the CPU does; one marked [✗] doesn't run.</p>
<p>The ROM folder is <span class="path">~/Library/Application Support/Lemus/ROMs</span> on macOS and <span class="path">%APPDATA%\Lemus\ROMs</span> on Windows, shared by every Lemus plugin. ROMs in a plugin's top bar shows the folder in use and what the plugin found in it, with full paths, and can point it elsewhere. ROMs are matched by SHA1, under any name, loose, in subfolders or in zips: MAME sets work as they are. Adding one later restores the whole machine in an open session.</p>

<h2 id="artwork" class="h3">Panel artwork</h2>
<p>Makers' logos and model names are trademarks, so their places on the panel are blank. Each place takes an SVG or PNG of your own, as MAME keeps artwork: one file per place, named after it (<span class="mono">maker</span>, <span class="mono">model</span>…), in <span class="path">artwork/&lt;machine&gt;/</span> in the ROM folder, or in <span class="path">artwork/&lt;machine&gt;.zip</span>. A file is drawn as it looks, scaled to fit its place with its proportions kept; only what it draws counts, not a page's margins or a PNG's transparent border, and transparent parts show the panel. ROMs in the top bar lists each place with its file name, its size in millimetres and the PNG size in pixels that stays sharp, opens the folder, and reads it again. Artwork stays in the ROM folder: sessions and saved states don't carry it. The place names are in the plugin's guide.</p>
<p>An older plugin's SVG file, with a slot for each place, still works for the places that have no file of their own.</p>

<h2 id="updates" class="h3">Update check</h2>
<p>Once a day, while a plugin window is open, the plugin fetches one small file from lemusdev.org and sends nothing about you. When a new version is out, a window lists what changed: Download, Remind me later (again in a week) or Skip this version (until the next one). The bar shows the update until it is installed. About › Check for updates once a day turns it all off.</p>

<h2 id="mailing-list" class="h3">Mailing list</h2>
<p>The mailing list carries release announcements only. The form at the foot of each page adds an address after it is confirmed by email. The list is held by Kit, which sends the mail; every email has an unsubscribe link.</p>

<h2 id="contact" class="h3">Forum and contact</h2>
<p>Questions, problems and bug reports go on the forum, <a href="https://lemus.discourse.group">lemus.discourse.group</a>. With a problem, give the plugin and version (in About), your system, your DAW and what you did. Bug reports are also taken as <a href="https://github.com/lemus-dev/lemus/issues">GitHub issues</a>.</p>
<p>Anything that can't be public, security issues included: <a href="mailto:hello@lemusdev.org">hello@lemusdev.org</a>.</p>
</Stub>
</Base>

